  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable but mainly dry through the weekend. \n\nSome patchy fog mainly for wind sheltered valleys will be possible \nin spots across the area this morning. \n\nCooler but dry today with high pressure passing through the eastern \nGreat lakes. Highs today will average a bit below normal with upper \n50s across the higher terrain, to low to mid 60s elsewhere. \n\nHigh pressure moves off into New England by Sunday morning. \nSoutherly flow on the western flank of the departing ridge will \nencourage a modest warming trend across the region. An approaching \nfront may even support a few afternoon showers by Sunday afternoon, \nmainly across the North Country. Otherwise...temperatures will nudge \nup to near normal with highs in the mid to upper 60s near 70F.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures with frost potential \nearly next week.\n\nA potent shortwave trough is advertised to track across eastern \nCanada and the Northeast through early next week. Meanwhile...a \nsharp cold front will race across the Lower Lakes Sunday night with \na bit of a better chances for some showers. A chilly airmass will \nthen follow behind the front sending H850 T's plunging into the \nsingle negative digits (-1C to -3C) by Monday evening. Given the \nchilly airmass aloft moving across the warm lake waters and some \nlimited remaining shallow moisture it is likely we will see some \nlake induced clouds and maybe even a few showers. The main story \nthough will be the early fall chill in the air on Monday, with \nafternoon highs only expected in the upper 40s to 50s. \n\nWith the loss of daytime heating and the surface high moving nearly \noverhead Monday night, mercury reading will fall off into the 30s. \nIt's even possible that in the coldest locations we could see \nreadings in the upper 20s by Tuesday morning, especially well inland \nfrom the lakes. While this is still a few days out there is the \npotential for frost Monday night. \n\nSurface high moves east of the area Tuesday with southerly return \nflow developing over the region. This will once again bring about a \nwarming trend ahead of the next system expected later in the week.",
